1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:00,734
In 2003,

2
00:00:00,734 --> 00:00:07,159
a voting machine in Belgium gave one candidate four thousand and ninety-six extra votes.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,477
The total didn't add up, so officials recounted.

4
00:00:10,477 --> 00:00:11,949
The extra votes vanished.

5
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,464
Investigators ruled out fraud and software bugs.

6
00:00:15,464 --> 00:00:16,971
The leading explanation:

7
00:00:16,971 --> 00:00:19,734
one bit in the machine's memory had flipped.

8
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:21,559
Here's the giveaway.

9
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:24,896
In binary, each bit is worth double the one before.

10
00:00:24,896 --> 00:00:26,336
One, two, four, eight.

11
00:00:26,336 --> 00:00:30,589
The thirteenth bit is worth exactly four thousand and ninety-six.

12
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:32,132
What flips a bit?

13
00:00:32,132 --> 00:00:36,572
Very often, a particle from a cosmic-ray shower, crashing into the chip.

14
00:00:37,083 --> 00:00:39,684
What else can a cosmic ray do to your computer?

15
00:00:39,684 --> 00:00:41,122
Full story on the channel.
